.TITLE "cat"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Concatenates and displays file contents on macOS. This command reads one or
more files sequentially and writes their contents to standard output. It is
commonly used to display the contents of small files, concatenate multiple
files into one, or create new files from standard input.

.DIV "ALIAS"
**type**

.DIV "TLDR"
**cat __file.txt__**
Displays the content of a single __file.txt__ to standard output.
> __file.txt__: The path to the file to display.

**cat __file1.txt__ __file2.txt__**
Concatenates and displays the contents of __file1.txt__ and __file2.txt__.
> __file1.txt__: The path to the first file. | __file2.txt__: The path to the second file.

**cat __file1.txt__ >> __output.txt__**
Appends the content of __file1.txt__ to __output.txt__.
> __file1.txt__: The path to the input file. | __output.txt__: The path to the output file.

**cat -n __file.txt__**
Displays the content of __file.txt__ with line numbers.
> __-n__: number lines flag | __file.txt__: The path to the file to display.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
While `cat` is useful for quickly viewing file contents, for larger files,
`less` or `more` are often preferred as they allow pagination. `cat` is
frequently used in combination with pipes (`|`) to feed file contents
as input to other commands.

.DIV "SEE ALSO"
**mac:less** **mac:grep**

documentation: https://ss64.com/osx/cat.html
